# Dispatch Policies

In this notebook you evolve a dispatch policy for a small job queue. A
simulator runs the queue under each policy and counts the jobs that finish by
their deadline. Genetic search reports each generation as it finishes. You run
one search until it stops improving, then cancel a second search partway
through a generation and read what the result keeps. The notebook runs offline
and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Simulate the queue

Each job has a duration, a due time, and a priority. A policy decides the order
in which one worker runs the jobs.

- `:fifo` runs jobs in arrival order.
- `:priority` runs the highest priority first.
- `:deadline` runs the earliest due time first.

```elixir
queue = [
  %{id: :report, duration: 4, due: 10, priority: 4},
  %{id: :payment, duration: 1, due: 1, priority: 3},
  %{id: :confirm, duration: 2, due: 3, priority: 2},
  %{id: :archive, duration: 1, due: 8, priority: 1}
]

simulate = fn policy, queue ->
  ordered =
    case policy do
      :fifo -> queue
      :priority -> Enum.sort_by(queue, & &1.priority, :desc)
      :deadline -> Enum.sort_by(queue, & &1.due)
    end

  {trace, _elapsed} =
    Enum.map_reduce(ordered, 0, fn job, elapsed ->
      finished = elapsed + job.duration
      {{job.id, finished <= job.due}, finished}
    end)

  %{order: Enum.map(trace, &elem(&1, 0)), on_time: Enum.count(trace, &elem(&1, 1))}
end

Enum.map([:fifo, :priority, :deadline], &{&1, simulate.(&1, queue)})
```

```text
[
  fifo: %{order: [:report, :payment, :confirm, :archive], on_time: 2},
  priority: %{order: [:report, :payment, :confirm, :archive], on_time: 2},
  deadline: %{order: [:payment, :confirm, :archive, :report], on_time: 4}
]
```

The arrival order already matches priority order here, so `:fifo` and
`:priority` tie at two on-time jobs. `:deadline` finishes all four on time.

## Build the search

The genome is a policy atom. The starting population holds `:fifo` and
`:priority`, and the mutation always proposes `:deadline`. With
`mutation_rate: 1`, every child is mutated. `elitism: 0` keeps no parent
unchanged, so each generation is all new children.

`patience: 1` stops the search after one generation with no new best.
`on_progress` receives events while the search runs. This one prints a line
after each generation and ignores the per-case and per-candidate events.

```elixir
report = fn
  %{event: :generation_evaluated} = snapshot ->
    policies = Enum.map(snapshot.population, & &1.value)

    IO.puts(
      "Generation #{snapshot.generation} #{inspect(policies)} complete? #{snapshot.complete?}, " <>
        "best #{snapshot.best.value} (#{snapshot.best.evaluation.selected_score})"
    )

  _event ->
    :ok
end

dispatch = fn runner, opts ->
  Tune.new(:fifo, run: runner, on_progress: report)
  |> Tune.data(validation: [queue])
  |> Tune.score(:on_time, & &1.on_time)
  |> Tune.search(
    :genetic,
    [
      initial_population: [:fifo, :priority, :fifo],
      mutate: fn _policy, rng -> {:deadline, rng} end,
      mutation_rate: 1,
      elitism: 0,
      generations: 5,
      patience: 1
    ] ++ opts
  )
  |> Tune.limit(evaluations: 9, seed: 12)
  |> Tune.tune()
end
```

## Run to the patience limit

```elixir
{:ok, winner} = dispatch.(simulate, [])

IO.puts("Selected #{winner.value}. Stop reason #{winner.stop_reason}, complete? #{winner.complete?}")
```

```text
Generation 0 [:fifo, :priority, :fifo] complete? true, best fifo (2.0)
Generation 1 [:deadline, :deadline, :deadline] complete? true, best deadline (4.0)
Generation 2 [:deadline, :deadline, :deadline] complete? true, best deadline (4.0)
Selected deadline. Stop reason patience, complete? true
```

Generation 1 finds `:deadline`. Generation 2 does not beat it, so `patience: 1`
ends the search. A patience stop counts as a complete search.

## Cancel a generation

Pass a zero-argument `cancel` function to stop a search from outside. Tune
checks it during the search. Here a counter tracks runner calls, and `cancel`
returns `true` once the fifth simulation has run. That point falls in the
middle of generation 1.

```elixir
runs = :counters.new(1, [])

counted = fn policy, queue ->
  :counters.add(runs, 1, 1)
  simulate.(policy, queue)
end

{:ok, cancelled} = dispatch.(counted, cancel: fn -> :counters.get(runs, 1) >= 5 end)

IO.puts("Selected #{cancelled.value}. Stop reason #{cancelled.stop_reason}, complete? #{cancelled.complete?}")
```

```text
Generation 0 [:fifo, :priority, :fifo] complete? true, best fifo (2.0)
Generation 1 [:deadline, :deadline] complete? false, best fifo (2.0)
Selected fifo. Stop reason cancelled, complete? false
```

The snapshot for generation 1 has `complete?: false` and only two of its three
members. The result keeps `:fifo`, the best candidate found before the interrupted generation.

## Read the interrupted generation

The history shows what happened inside generation 1:

```elixir
for candidate <- cancelled.history do
  evaluation = candidate.evaluation

  IO.puts(
    "#{candidate.id} g#{candidate.lineage.generation} #{candidate.value}: " <>
      "eligible? #{evaluation.eligible?}, stop #{evaluation.stop_reason}"
  )
end

:ok
```

```text
0 g0 fifo: eligible? true, stop completed
1 g0 priority: eligible? true, stop completed
2 g0 fifo: eligible? true, stop completed
3 g1 deadline: eligible? true, stop completed
4 g1 deadline: eligible? false, stop cancelled
```

Candidate `"3"` finished and scored 4, more than `:fifo`. Candidate `"4"` was
cancelled, so it is not eligible. Tune does not promote a winner from a
generation it could not finish. It returns the best candidate found before the
interrupted generation, and the full history stays in the result for review.

